# Target C — C93 strict secondary-separator frontier

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

Full proof: [target_c_strict_secondary_separator_budget_2026-09-01.md](../../reports/target_c_strict_secondary_separator_budget_2026-09-01.md).

For a non-chain active diameter h with C83 chain interval length `ell_h`, let `k_h`
be the total number of trains separating its endpoints. Exact train coordinates give
`k_h>=D`. Equality would force every oriented separator vector to be parallel to the
diameter vector. Since the interval is nonempty, one separator is a C80 chain vector
`+-u`, so h would be parallel to the unique chain diameter `za`, impossible.
Therefore

`k_h>=D+1`,

and at least `D+1-ell_h` separators lie outside the D-track chain atom.

C87 supplies two selected diameter orientations over each of its D staircase edges,
and C88 gives

`sum_e ell_e=D(D+1)/2`.

Hence the exact selected diameter/non-chain-train incidence budget is

`>=D(D+1)`.

Some non-chain train therefore separates at least

`D(D+1)/(T-D)`

selected active diameter pairs; C90 classifies the resulting train-cut graph as a
caterpillar forest.

C93 improves C88 by exactly `2D` and removes the equality-at-D separator loophole. The
leading asymptotic macroscopic-cut coefficient is unchanged.